.TH ISEKUR 1 "November 8, 2026" "iSekur 0.5.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
isekur \- colorful terminal IRC client with a built-in webchat
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B isekur
.RB [ \-c
.IR config ]
.RB [ \-d | \-\-daemon ]
.RB [ \-a | \-\-attach ]
.RB [ \-l | \-\-list ]
.RB [ \-\-web | \-\-no\-web ]
.RB [ \-\-bnc ]
.RB [ \-\-hash\-password ]
.RB [ \-v | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B isekur
is a terminal IRC client with multiple servers and tabs, 24-bit color themes,
TLS, IRCv3 and SASL. Its optional webchat mirrors the running session in a
browser over HTTPS and WebSocket, so you can keep chatting from elsewhere.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " file"
Use
.I file
instead of
.IR ~/.config/isekur/isekur.conf .
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-daemon
Start in the background without a terminal; attach with
.B \-\-attach
or the webchat, which this mode always tries to start (it needs
.B [web] user
and
.BR password_hash "). Output goes to"
.I isekur.log
next to the config file
.RI ( work.log
for
.IR work.conf ).
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-attach
Bring a detached iSekur back to this terminal. With none running for the
config, a new one starts (its first window says it's a new session). Starting
.B isekur
while one is running with the same config attaches too. Several run side by
side with one config each
.RB ( "isekur \-c ~/.config/isekur/work.conf" ).
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List the iSekurs running from the default config's directory (and
.BR \-c 's),
each with the command that attaches it.
.TP
.BR \-\-web ", " \-\-no\-web
Start the webchat, or don't, regardless of
.B [web] autostart
(off by default). While running,
.B /web start
and
.B /web stop
do the same.
.TP
.B \-\-bnc
Run in the background as a bouncer only: no terminal and no webchat
.RB ( \-\-web
adds it). Your other IRC clients connect to the
.B [bnc]
address and port with the server password
.IR user / network : password
and share this session. Logged in with no network (or one iSekur doesn't have),
.B /msg *isekur
lists, picks or adds one. A
.B bnc.motd
file in the config's folder is the clients' MOTD, read at each login. It needs
.B [bnc] user
and
.BR password_hash ;
.B /bnc password
sets the hash from inside iSekur.
.B \-\-attach
still works. While running,
.B /bnc start
and
.B /bnc stop
turn the BNC on and off.
.TP
.B \-\-hash\-password
Read a password and print a
.B password_hash
line for the config
.RB ( [web] " or " [bnc] ).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Alt+1..9, Alt+0
Go to tab 1..10.
.TP
.B Alt+Up/Down, Ctrl+P/N
Previous / next tab.
.TP
.B Alt+Left/Right
Previous / next server tab, or a tab of no network (cmd, shells).
.TP
.B Ctrl+A, Alt+A
Next tab with activity.
.TP
.B Alt+S
Lock the terminal and the webchat now (Option+S on a Mac), like
.BR /lock ;
with no lock password set yet it says how to set one.
.TP
.B Alt+D, Ctrl+Z
Detach (Option+D on a Mac): iSekur keeps running in the background, and
.B isekur \-\-attach
brings it back. Closing the terminal or a dropped SSH connection detaches too.
.TP
.B F1, Alt+K
The key list (\fB/keys\fR): every binding drawn as keycaps, by group, including your own \fB[keys]\fR.
.TP
.B Ctrl+X
Next server tab.
.TP
.B Ctrl+G, Alt+J
Find a tab: type part of a channel, nick or network name; Enter goes there.
.TP
.B Ctrl+W
Close the tab.
.TP
.B PgUp, PgDn
Scroll.
.TP
.B Ctrl+L, Ctrl+T (or Alt+L, F2, Alt+T)
Show / hide the nicklist / the topic bar (Option+L, Option+T on a Mac).
.TP
.B F3
Toggle the mouse. With it on, a drag in the chat copies the text (OSC 52) and Shift+drag is the terminal's own selection; on the topic bar the wheel scrolls it, a click unfolds it and a double click opens it in a box to edit (when you may change it). Links in messages and the topic are terminal hyperlinks (OSC 8): Cmd+click or Ctrl+click opens them, with Shift when the terminal gives the click to iSekur. A right click on a server tab opens a menu: connect, or reconnect and disconnect, hide or show its channels and queries in the tab bar (they stay reachable with Ctrl+N, Ctrl+P, Ctrl+A and /win; /fold does the same), and close (it asks first while connected). A right click on a channel tab opens a menu: quiet mode on or off, clear, add to or remove from autojoin, and close (it asks first when that leaves the channel). A right click on a nick in the nicklist opens a menu: query, whois, CTCP, ignore, and op, voice, kick and ban when your channel rank allows them.
.TP
.B Tab
Complete a command after \fB/\fR or \fB/help\fR, the config key after \fB/set\fR, or a nick (matches are listed as you type; a nick first on the line gets \fBcomplete_suffix\fR, ":" by default).
.TP
.B Home / End, Ctrl+E, Ctrl+U / Ctrl+K
Start / end of line, end of line, delete to start / end.
.TP
.B Alt+Backspace
Delete the previous word.
.TP
.B Cmd+Backspace
Delete to start, when the terminal forwards Cmd (CSI u).
.PP
Actions can be rebound in the
.B [keys]
section, also live with
.BR "/set keys.next_tab M\-n" .
.SH COMMANDS
.B /server host
.RB [ port "] [" \-tls "] [" \-noverify ],
.BR /connect ", " /disconnect ", " /join ", " /part ", " /msg ", " /query ,
.BR /me ", " /notice ", " /nick ", " /topic ", " /quiet ", " /close ", " /win " N, " /raw ", " /rawlog ,
.BR /ctcp ", " /theme ", " /keys ", " /set " [section.]key [value], " /save ", " /reload ,
.BR /ignore " [mask], " /unignore " mask, " /hilight " [add|del] word, " /grep " [-all] text, " /clear ", " /quit " (this tab's server), " /exit " [-y] (close iSekur; with a lock password set, it asks for it).
.BR /reply " nick text, " /react " emoji [nick], " /redact ", " /notify " [add|del] nick, " /setname ,
.BR /register " [email] and " /verify " use IRCv3 where the server has it.
.B /dcc get
downloads the newest file sent to you (DCC SEND) into
.I dcc_dir
in the background, resuming a broken one;
.B /dcc send
nick file offers one;
.B /dcc
lists the transfers,
.B /dcc close
n refuses or cancels one. With
.I dcc_web
on, the webchat downloads what's in
.IR dcc_dir .
.B /e2e nick
asks for a password and starts an encrypted query with another iSekur user, who accepts with
.B /e2e
and your nick and the same password (AES-256-GCM, key from scrypt, kept in memory only);
.B /e2e off
ends it.
.B /e2e #channel
encrypts a channel for everyone who types the same password (no handshake; others see +iSE lines).
Channel operators also get
.BR /massop ", " /massdeop ", " /massvoice ", " /massdevoice ", " /masskick ,
.BR /lockdown ", " /unlock ", " /kickban " (" /kb "), " /tempban ", " /chanstats
and
.BR /topicrotate .
.B /shell
opens a terminal in a tab after this machine's login;
.BI /attach " host " [ config ]
runs
.B ssh \-t
.I host
.B isekur
.RB [ \-c
.IR config ]
.B \-\-attach
in one, showing that machine's iSekur inside this one (Alt+D there detaches it).
Several iSekurs run side by side with one config each
.RB ( "isekur \-c work.conf" );
.I config
picks one.
.B /plugin
opens the plugin window (installed and available plugins, their settings per network and channel),
.B /plugin list
lists the Lua plugins,
.B /plugin available
the catalog's,
.BI "/plugin install " name
fetches one (checked by sha256),
.B update
and
.B remove
likewise;
.BI "/plugin enable " name\fR[\fB@\fInetwork\fR[\fB/\fI#channel\fR]]
and
.B disable
turn one on or off there,
.BI "/plugin set " "name key value"
sets its options,
.BI "/plugin reload " name
loads it again.
Other commands are sent to the server unchanged.
.B /help
lists them in the client;
.BI /help " command"
explains one, with examples.
.SH FIFO
With
.B fifo = on
in the config (or
.BR "/set fifo on" ),
iSekur creates a named pipe and reads it: every line written to it runs as if typed.
Each running iSekur has its own pipe (see FILES); write to the one for the instance you want.
.PP
.nf
.B printf '%s\(rsn' '/msg bob hi' > ~/.config/isekur/fifo
.fi
.PP
A line runs in the active tab, unless it starts with a target and a
.B |
.RI ( net / #chan | text ,
.IR net | /cmd ,
.IR #chan | text ,
or
.IR N | text
for tab N, as in
.BR /win ).
The network name is the server name (see
.BR /servers ),
matched without regard to case. A prefix with no space must be a target: when no server or open tab matches, the line is refused with a message in the status tab and nothing is sent. A prefix with a space is plain text.
.PP
Each line is decoded:
.B \(rsn
ends it,
.B \(rsxHH
is any byte (\(rsx03 color),
.B \(rse
the escape,
.B \(rs\(rs
a backslash, and
.BR \(rsb ", " \(rsi ", " \(rsu ", " \(rss ", " \(rsm ", " \(rsv ", " \(rso
are bold, italics, underline, strikethrough, monospace, reverse and reset.
Other backslashes stay as typed.
.PP
The pipe is owner-only (mode 0600), ignored while iSekur is locked, and removed at exit.
Not available on native Windows.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/isekur/isekur.conf
Configuration (INI). Written with every setting on the first start; an empty value means the
default. See
.I iSekur.conf.example
in the data directory.
.TP
.I ~/.config/isekur/themes/*.theme
User themes; also looked up in
.I ./themes
and the installed data directory.
.TP
.I ~/.config/isekur/plugins/<name>.lua
Lua plugins, sandboxed; their options are in
.RI [plugin. name ]
sections of the config and their saved data in
.IR <name>.data .
.TP
.I <log_dir>/<server>/<name>.log
Chat logs when
.B log = on
is set; earlier days are kept as
.IR <name>.YYYY\-MM\-DD.log .
.TP
.I ~/.config/isekur/isekur.sock
The socket
.B \-\-attach
uses, only for your user, named after the config
.RI ( work.sock
for
.IR work.conf ).
Its output in the background goes to
.I isekur.log
.RI ( work.log )
in the same directory.
.TP
.I ~/.config/isekur/web.sessions
Hashed webchat login sessions
.RI ( work.web.sessions
for
.IR work.conf ).
.TP
.I ~/.config/isekur/fifo
The FIFO pipe, with
.B fifo = on
(
.I work.fifo
beside
.IR work.conf ).
Or wherever
.B fifo_path
says.
